WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.460
The first film put dates on its fossil flags and on the last cliff.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.440
But how can a rock say when a bone was buried, with no one there to see?

3
00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:15.703
Fossils are rarely dated directly.

4
00:00:17.103 --> 00:00:19.383
Often, minerals in the rocks around them are.

5
00:00:20.503 --> 00:00:21.803
A favourite is zircon.

6
00:00:23.043 --> 00:00:27.923
It crystallizes in molten rock, and an eruption carries it into volcanic ash.

7
00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:33.367
As zircon crystallizes, it takes up uranium but almost no lead.

8
00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:37.667
So nearly all the lead inside it comes from later decay.

9
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:43.827
Each uranium atom has the same small chance of decaying in any given year.

10
00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:48.387
So half the parent atoms decay in a fixed time, the half-life.

11
00:00:49.627 --> 00:00:51.467
It is constant for each isotope.

12
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:59.007
Watch the grid. On average, after one half-life, half the uranium has become lead.

13
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:02.287
After the next, half of what was left.

14
00:01:03.447 --> 00:01:05.767
The crystal keeps time from the moment it formed.

15
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.310
Suppose the crystal stayed closed and started with no lead.

16
00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.710
Then the parent left and the lead built up count the half-lives.

17
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.390
That count gives the age.

18
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.400
Now take a stack of rock layers.

19
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.280
A bed of fossils lies between two layers of volcanic ash.

20
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.560
Each ash can be dated by its zircons.

21
00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:33.973
In undisturbed layers, younger ones settle on top of older ones.

22
00:01:35.093 --> 00:01:36.793
So the oldest lies at the bottom.

23
00:01:38.113 --> 00:01:40.733
How old, then, are the fossils between the two ashes?

24
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:50.353
Younger than the ash below, and older than the ash above.

25
00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:54.113
The answer is a bracket, not a single date.

26
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:57.737
Now look at the oldest known fossil of a group.

27
00:01:58.897 --> 00:02:00.797
It shows the group was here by then.

28
00:02:01.957 --> 00:02:03.717
It does not show when the group began.

29
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.500
Earlier members may never have been preserved, or may not have been found yet.

30
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.440
So a first fossil is a floor, a minimum age.

31
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:15.720
The group may be older.

32
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.700
Oxygen in the air is a different kind of date.

33
00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:26.120
The start of the Great Oxidation Event was bracketed by dated volcanic rocks, from

34
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.120
both sides.

35
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.890
The clock must also fit the age.

36
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:32.790
Carbon-14 decays quickly.

37
00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.530
Long before the age of dinosaurs, too little is left to measure.

38
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.220
So dinosaur bones are not carbon-dated.

39
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.380
Where volcanic ash lies near them, slower clocks date those layers.

40
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.760
Elsewhere, other methods tie the rocks to dated layers.

41
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:54.223
Two kinds of date run through this lesson.

42
00:02:55.643 --> 00:02:57.823
Dated layers around a fossil give a bracket.

43
00:02:59.083 --> 00:03:01.323
A group's oldest known fossil gives a floor.
